    },
    "depth": 0,
    "payload": {
      "content": "The proposed architecture, KerisRDNet, extends InceptionResNetV2 \npretrained on ImageNet by incorporating two IRD blocks. The convo-\nlutional layers of the backbone were frozen during the initial training \nphase, while the IRD blocks were designed to capture the elongated \ngeometry and fine pamor textures of the keris blades by enlarging \nthe receptive fields without additional pooling and improving the \nmultiscale sensitivity."
    },
    "control_plane": {},
    "parent_node_id": null,
    "segment_index": 0,
    "segment_count": 1
  },
  {
    "node_id": "p2_b8",
    "sequence_id": 19,
    "node_type": "paragraph",
    "strategy": "translate",
    "metadata": {
      "bboxes": [
        {
          "x0": 306.60400390625,
          "y0": 588.8131103515625,
          "x1": 561.0055541992188,
          "y1": 683.336181640625,
          "is_normalized": false
        }
      ],
      "pages": [
        2
      ],
      "provider_native_id": "8",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "layout_reading_order": 18,
      "semantic_origin": "pdf_text"
    },
    "depth": 0,
    "payload": {
      "content": "Each IRD block applied four parallel depthwise-separable convo-\nlutions, 1 × 1 (dilation 1), 3 × 3 (dilation 2), 3 × 3 (dilation 3), and \n5 × 5 (dilation 4), with pre-activation (BatchNorm, ReLU) in each \nbranch. The outputs were concatenated, followed by dropout (𝑝=\n0.2), residual scaling (×0.1), and a skip connection with an optional \n1 × 1 projection when the channels differed. A squeeze-and-excitation \nmodule (reduction ratio 16) recalibrated the channel weights, further \nenhancing the discriminative capacity."
    },
    "control_plane": {},
    "parent_node_id": null,
    "segment_index": 0,
    "segment_count": 1
  },
